WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.480
You may wonder why Geena Davis doesn't&nbsp;
work as much in front of the camera.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.920
Not only has she become more selective about her&nbsp;
roles, Davis is also a producer and has founded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:16.080
a research institute and film festival. She's&nbsp;
trying to change the film industry for the better.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.040
1992's A League of Their Own was&nbsp;
a massive success, and Geena Davis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.240
credits the film for helping her claim the&nbsp;
feminist label. But throughout the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.040
Davis worked on a string of movies that couldn't&nbsp;
replicate the success of A League of Their Own.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:36.000
Later that same year, Davis appeared in&nbsp;
the movie Hero opposite Dustin Hoffman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.440
which faltered despite the strength of the&nbsp;
cast. In 1994, she starred in the drama Angie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.880
a film that performed poorly at the box office and&nbsp;
failed to garner a positive response from critics.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.040
Next up was Speechless, a movie that&nbsp;
starred Davis as a political speechwriter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.640
who falls in love with a speechwriter working for&nbsp;
a rival campaign. The film was panned by critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.640
and her next role certainly didn't&nbsp;
help to revive her career, either.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.200
In 1995, Davis starred as Morgan&nbsp;
Adams in Cutthroat Island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.160
which critic Daniel Barnes summed up as:

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.480
"A chaotic jumble of bad action,&nbsp;
juvenile comedy and chilly romance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.880
all wrapped in a cheesecloth of&nbsp;
limp, lifeless pirate cliches."

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.920
Appearing in several films with mediocre&nbsp;
reviews was definitely a setback for Davis.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.680
In recent years, Geena Davis has found that&nbsp;
she has a better track record of landing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:33.920
fulfilling roles in TV shows than in films.&nbsp;
From 2000 to 2001, she had her own series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.880
The Geena Davis Show. Later, she starred as&nbsp;
President Allen in the series Commander in Chief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:45.200
and she also appeared on the first season of&nbsp;
The Exorcist as Angela Rance. And in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.080
she played Sandy Devereaux St. Clair&nbsp;
on the beloved Netflix series GLOW.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.960
Davis says that she enjoys working in TV, but&nbsp;
investing so much time and energy into shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.600
that have been canceled is discouraging. These&nbsp;
roles turned out to be short-lived. The Geena&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:05.040
Davis Show and Commander in Chief only lasted&nbsp;
one season apiece, and GLOW was canceled after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:10.400
three seasons — Davis's final appearance on&nbsp;
the show turned out to be the series finale.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.240
Davis told AARP:

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.120
"I was so bummed when ABC's Commander in&nbsp;
Chief went off the air. As President Allen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.520
I had a very short administration."

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.200
Unfortunately, she's struggled&nbsp;
to find a long-running TV role.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:30.320
Geena Davis became a mother in her forties. In&nbsp;
2001, she married Reza Jarrahy, and the couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:36.720
had their first child, a daughter in 2002. And&nbsp;
in 2004, Davis gave birth to their twin sons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.320
Eventually, Davis and Jarrahy separated,&nbsp;
officially going their separate ways in 2018.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.320
Davis says that she had always wanted to&nbsp;
become a mother, but when she was younger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.240
she felt like the timing just wasn't&nbsp;
right. Now, she sees the benefits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:54.400
having children later in life, and she&nbsp;
feels that she made the right decision.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.920
Davis told The Guardian:

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.920
"I always felt lucky that I had my kids&nbsp;
late, because I just feel like I changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.160
so much. I always knew I wanted kids,&nbsp;
but what I was doing waiting that long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.960
I don't know. But it's been&nbsp;
great. And twins are fun!"

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.000
"Life is pain. Get used to it!"

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.320
Geena Davis hasn't retired from acting, but&nbsp;
since 2004, she's taken up another cause.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:24.320
She currently runs the Geena Davis&nbsp;
Institute on Gender in Media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:28.160
and she's on a mission to create&nbsp;
more roles for women in Hollywood.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.600
Davis explained to Vogue:

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.280
"The reason I started this whole research&nbsp;
institute is because I found out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.560
people absolutely had no idea that kids'&nbsp;
media was so gender-biased. And before I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.720
watched it with my daughter, I was sure&nbsp;
it was fine. I was appalled to learn the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.640
truth and I decided I was gonna bring&nbsp;
it up in my daily life in Hollywood."

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.360
While Davis was watching&nbsp;
children's shows with her daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.600
she noticed that there were far more&nbsp;
male characters than female characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.320
but when she brought it up at meetings, no one&nbsp;
seemed to realize the extent of the problem.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.600
"Our research shows there's a&nbsp;
major gender gap in kids' media.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.280
Male characters outweigh&nbsp;
female characters two to one."

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.040
Davis decided that analyzing&nbsp;
the data was the answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.960
so she founded the Institute and&nbsp;
spearheaded the largest research project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.720
concerning gender depictions in&nbsp;
TV and movies ever conducted.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:21.680
The disaappointing results confirmed her&nbsp;
suspicions — women simply didn't get enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:26.080
representation. She's been focused&nbsp;
on changing this issue ever since.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.680
"If girls can see female characters taking up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.680
half of the space, then they will&nbsp;
think of that in their real life."

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:39.040
A large part of Geena Davis' work with&nbsp;
the Institute concerns gathering data on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.080
gender disparities in the media.&nbsp;
Plenty of people are interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.080
in this issue, so why doesn't Davis&nbsp;
spend more time promoting her research?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.240
She says that she's more effective at&nbsp;
pushing for change behind the scenes.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:51.440
Davis told Interview:

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.360
"We go meet with every studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.240
every guild, every network, every production&nbsp;
company and share it with them, privately. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:03.600
don't really bust anybody publicly. It's much&nbsp;
more efficient if I can impact the creators."

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.840
And in an interview with Glamour, Davis&nbsp;
explained that her goals for the Institute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.320
don't necessarily revolve around raising&nbsp;
awareness of the issue with the public.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.000
"I give speeches and interviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.960
and we release data to the public. But the&nbsp;
main goal is not to educate the populace."

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.760
She feels that public pressure isn't necessarily&nbsp;
the key to making change in the entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.320
industry — instead, she has to go directly to&nbsp;
the people in charge who can make it happen.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.920
Besides meeting with directors and screenwriters&nbsp;
to push for more female representation on screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:37.600
what else has Geena Davis been up to&nbsp;
that occupies so much of her time?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:41.440
She's also working on initiatives to&nbsp;
encourage more diversity in Hollywood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:44.240
And she's taking things into her own hands.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.800
"BFF is an exciting one-of-a-kind&nbsp;
initiative that will champion women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.440
and diversity in all aspects of film."

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.880
In 2015, Davis co-founded the Bentonville&nbsp;
Film Festival, a non-profit film festival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:02.720
that showcases movies directed by women, people&nbsp;
of color, and members of the LGBTQ community.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.000
The Bentonville Film Foundation,&nbsp;
which hosts the festival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.120
also provides ongoing, year-round support to&nbsp;
filmmakers who are generally underrepresented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.960
in Hollywood. Davis wants to give&nbsp;
opportunities to talented directors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.960
who might not get the attention they&nbsp;
deserve in mainstream film circles.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.320
Davis told The Guardian:

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.080
"Oh, we want to change the&nbsp;
world! Our goal is very simple:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.040
the storytellers and people on screen should&nbsp;
reflect the population, which is half female&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:35.360
and incredibly diverse. It's not like: 'Wow, what&nbsp;
a far-fetched idea!' It just makes total sense."

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:39.680
"We're always looking for great films&nbsp;
that are inclusive, that reflect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.040
society as it is, half female and very diverse."

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.880
Over the course of her career, Geena Davis has&nbsp;
gotten pickier about the roles she's accepted.&nbsp;

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:51.360
Before she commits to playing&nbsp;
a particular character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:56.080
she always asks herself what the women in the&nbsp;
audience would think of that particular role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.560
She wants to play characters who are genuinely&nbsp;
interesting and complex rather than settling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:04.880
for parts that don't speak to her — but&nbsp;
unfortunately, she's finding it difficult.

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:06.240
Davis told Interview:

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:09.920
"I really want to get some good&nbsp;
parts. Now I have so much experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:13.600
I probably could be better than ever. I&nbsp;
just want them to come along every once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:18.160
in a while. It's okay if it takes two or three&nbsp;
years for something really good to come along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:21.440
but I don't want to wait ten years&nbsp;
for something great to come along.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:25.760
It's maddening. It's so frustrating.&nbsp;
It's completely embarrassing."

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:29.680
At this point, Davis doesn't want to take a&nbsp;
role just for the paycheck. But even if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:34.080
means waiting a long time in between movies,&nbsp;
she's willing to stick to her principles.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.600
Geena Davis said that once she turned forty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.080
she realized that she wasn't being offered&nbsp;
nearly as many roles as she was before.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:43.360
She told The Guardian:

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:47.120
"I fell off the cliff. I really did.&nbsp;
In the early stages of my career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:52.320
I was blithely going along thinking, 'Meryl&nbsp;
Streep, Jessica Lange and Sally Field, they're all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:58.240
making these great female-centric movies. And I'm&nbsp;
getting these great roles, really tippy-top roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:03.440
so things must be getting better for women.' But&nbsp;
suddenly, the great roles were incredibly scarce."

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.080
Age discrimination is an unfortunate reality&nbsp;
in Hollywood. While some believe that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:13.760
more roles in movies for older actresses today&nbsp;
than there used to be, Davis explained to AARP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.800
that this is a myth — despite the efforts of&nbsp;
actresses like herself, the problem persists.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:21.120
Naturally, Davis is frustrated by this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.480
but instead of simply retiring, she's still moving&nbsp;
forward and looking for the roles that she wants.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.640
In addition to acting, Geena&nbsp;
Davis is also an athlete.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:35.920
After learning new sports on film sets, she&nbsp;
decided that she wanted to train in archery.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:37.120
She told People:

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:41.360
"I thought, 'I want to take up a sport in&nbsp;
the real-life way and not the movie version,'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.240
because they can fake anything. Like my character&nbsp;
in A League of Their Own only hit home runs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:50.960
so I would do a nice swing, but the props&nbsp;
guys had a giant slingshot to send the ball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:55.360
over the fence with. So I thought, 'I want to&nbsp;
see if I can really learn something real.'"

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.760
"It turned out I actually was athletic so&nbsp;
that's when I took up all this other stuff."

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:04.000
Davis began working with a trainer and&nbsp;
started practicing for five hours per day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:09.840
six days a week — and she quickly found that she&nbsp;
was exceptionally talented at the sport. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:14.640
she was so skilled that she made it to the&nbsp;
semifinals for the 2000 Olympics in Sydney.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.120
She placed 24th and just missed&nbsp;
qualifying for the U.S. Olympic team.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.680
Today, Davis no longer competes in archery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:26.720
but she does still practice when she has the&nbsp;
time. She also took up another sport — rowing!

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:32.720
Geena Davis has consistently continued working,&nbsp;
but her recent releases haven't fared well. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.800
2020, Davis had a supporting role as Bobbie&nbsp;
in the crime drama Ava. Ava, a skilled and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:43.440
lethal assassin who works for a black ops&nbsp;
organization, is responsible for carrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:49.200
out hits on high profile figures across the globe.&nbsp;
But when one of her jobs takes a dangerous turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:53.840
suddenly the tables turn, and she's forced to&nbsp;
confront her mistakes and fight for her life.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:58.160
The plot was standard action-movie fare,&nbsp;
but neither critics nor audiences found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:03.200
it particularly compelling. In a review&nbsp;
for Decider, critic John Serba remarked:

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:06.160
"Ava is a violent, boilerplate Bourne-y thriller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:10.480
without a single original bone in its&nbsp;
body. File under waste of talent."

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.440
It certainly didn't help that the&nbsp;
movie was released in the midst of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:19.040
the COVID-19 pandemic — chances are, even fans&nbsp;
of Davis did not know that it was coming out.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:24.640
Geena Davis is always open to opportunities&nbsp;
that allow her to work behind the scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:29.840
if it means creating an empowering movie or&nbsp;
TV show. While she has not directed a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:33.360
Davis has certainly diversified&nbsp;
her career behind the scenes.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.440
From consulting with directors and encouraging&nbsp;
them to include more female characters in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:43.200
their scripts to working as a producer on&nbsp;
various projects, Davis has worn many hats.

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:49.120
In 2018, Davis served as an executive producer&nbsp;
on the documentary This Changes Everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:52.240
which explores gender bias&nbsp;
within Hollywood. Davis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:56.880
and many other actors, appear in the film&nbsp;
to share their experiences as actresses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:02.080
Davis unveils why she began researching&nbsp;
and tackling the issue on her own.

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:04.480
In addition, Davis produced the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:11.360
Mission Unstoppable with Miranda Cosgrove from&nbsp;
2019 through 2021. In Mission Unstoppable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:16.720
Cosgrove interviews women working on innovative&nbsp;
endeavors in fields like science, technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:21.840
engineering, and math — so it's easy to see&nbsp;
why Davis wanted to get onboard with the show.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:28.080
Geena Davis hasn't given up on finding interesting&nbsp;
new roles and projects. She has an upcoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:34.320
movie and a TV series lined up. And she'll&nbsp;
also be venturing into the world of reality TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:37.920
with her own series, I Can&nbsp;
By Friday, in which she'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.320
spend each episode learning difficult new&nbsp;
skills and attempting challenging stunts.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:45.920
Judging by her success at mastering&nbsp;
athletic talents on movie sets and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:51.280
becoming a legit archery champion, she probably&nbsp;
won't have much trouble! One thing is for sure:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:55.120
whether Davis is working her magic&nbsp;
behind the camera or on screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:59.680
she's determined to be a force for&nbsp;
change in Hollywood for years to come.

